#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060762} :activity/id #uuid "93095345-cb92-55a9-9d0f-c4b0bb65cabb" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050921 :topic/id #uuid "71d6e8f7-677c-5db5-8a47-c2151c322714" :topic/math-academy-id 260} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060762 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060964 :tutorial/content "Suppose we conduct a random sample of size $n=10$ from a normal population with the unknown population mean $\\mu$ and *known* population variance $\\sigma^2=9.$ After processing our results, we compute the mean of this sample using the usual methods and get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\overline{x}=12\n$$\n\n \n\nThis is an unbiased estimate of the population mean $\\mu.$ However, it is unsatisfactory because we have no information regarding its reliability.\n\nFor this reason, it's often more helpful to give a range of possible values for $\\mu.$ In other words, instead of reporting a single estimate $\\overline{x},$ we might instead report a so-called **confidence interval,** which is an interval of the form\n\n$$\n(\\overline{x}-E, \\: \\overline{x}+E)\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overline{x}$ is our estimate of the population mean, and\n\n- $E$ is some **margin of error**.\n\nIn addition, we need to give some indication regarding the reliability of our confidence interval. With this in mind, we will construct a $\\boldsymbol{95\\%}$ **confidence interval** for the population mean $\\mu$. The precise meaning of this will become clear shortly." :tutorial/id #uuid "f8ebcc7b-ddba-56d2-bb41-07916f3c2d09" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6232 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "55b16e12-fb99-528d-a98f-9e754fd27b3a" :step/math-academy-id 37842 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060769}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060763 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059823 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059382 :question/id #uuid "97c8f1dc-747e-5aa4-ab4f-eb809abb560e" :question/math-academy-id "e-10035"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb626c84-db31-5e1b-90cb-3f5d13ee0af5" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049014}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060050} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060053} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498533} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498542} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498559} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498580}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals From Large Samples"} :step/id #uuid "bc49103b-afb3-574a-8efd-6910f8c3b320" :step/math-academy-id 37861 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060764}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060764 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059824 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059383 :question/id #uuid "3189a42a-200e-5caa-bc76-6ac1f4ae424d" :question/math-academy-id "e-10039"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "27d20d28-3fba-5830-8bff-76e9a1c24bec" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060051} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060054} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498524} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498543} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498557} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498576}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals: Applications"} :step/id #uuid "32d40006-ed8e-530b-8e5f-6ec28697e1f4" :step/math-academy-id 37883} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060765 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060965 :tutorial/content "Up to now, we've assumed that the underlying population is normally distributed. However, if we have a *sufficiently large* sample, then according to the central limit theorem, we can use the approximation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\mu}{\\sigma/\\sqrt n} \\approx N(0,1).\n$$\n\nSo, even though we might not know the underlying distribution, we can still use our formula for the confidence interval of the population mean $\\mu{:}$\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} \\pm z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4b9c6c26-d589-5847-a384-c431ad44a125" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6245 :tutorial/title "Large Samples"} :step/id #uuid "043270f2-b372-59f5-98a2-64919b13fa7b" :step/math-academy-id 37884 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060763}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060766 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060966 :tutorial/content "It's important to be able to interpret confidence intervals correctly.\n\nImagine we conduct several independent random samples from a population. The mean of the sample will be different for each sample. Some values of $\\overline{x}$ might lie close to the population mean $\\mu,$ and some could lie further away.\n\nSuppose we construct a $90\\%$ confidence interval for each sample and plot them on the same diagram. We might get a picture similar to the one below.\n\n![](images/fc/fc8dd707751cd9e1cb0da1bf618b6d7aed740fab875d8d49c7c89fc09b77321e.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, we see that nine out of ten of our confidence intervals contain $\\mu,$ while one of them (the red one) does *not* contain the population mean $\\mu.$ \n\nThe idea is that if we construct many $90\\%$ confidence intervals, one for each sample, then approximately $90\\%$ of these confidence intervals will contain the population mean $\\mu.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d71a924b-5f1e-5a89-a56b-d540a4a53d74" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6247 :tutorial/title "Interpreting Confidence Intervals"} :step/id #uuid "5f0968c6-9ace-5735-9d92-61ff226b832e" :step/math-academy-id 37890 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060765}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060767 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059825 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059384 :question/id #uuid "3a166c80-cc70-5dd3-9dbe-2798dc882199" :question/math-academy-id "e-10046"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b90997d0-b563-5541-9fe2-36d7457da7b5" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049180} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049652}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060052} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060055} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498501} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498508} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498518} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498562} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498573}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals From Normal Populations"} :step/id #uuid "3461449b-03e6-5f5d-aff8-7cec85835701" :step/math-academy-id 37913 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060766}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060768 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060967 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a normal population with the unknown population mean $\\mu$ and *known* population variance $\\sigma^2.$ Then, for a given value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ a $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\left(\\overline{x} - z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}}, \\: \\overline{x} + z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}}\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2},$ and $Z \\sim N(0,1).$ \n\nThe endpoints of our confidence interval are called **confidence limits** and are given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} \\pm z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nEach part of the formula above has a name:\n\n- $\\overline{x}$ is an estimate of the population mean\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}}$ is the standard error of the mean\n\n- $z_{\\alpha/2}$ is the corresponding $z$-score\n\n- $E = z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}}$ is the margin of error\n\n![](images/58/581fc2da88e4419e78389a1b5eedb127f590641d0b7ee21c489ed85184795b13.svg)\n\nSo, our confidence interval can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\overset{―}{x}-[\\text{margin of error}], & \\,\\overset{―}{x}+[\\text{margin of error}]) \\\\ (\\overset{―}{x}-[\\text{[math]z[/math]-score}]\\cdot [\\text{standard error}], & \\,\\overset{―}{x}+[\\text{[math]z[/math]-score}]\\cdot [\\text{standard error}]) \\\\ (\\overset{―}{x}-{z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}, & \\,\\overset{―}{x}+{z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince computing a confidence interval amounts to finding the corresponding confidence limits, we will use the two terms interchangeably." :tutorial/id #uuid "cdae1125-df9d-525c-823d-64f129c3ab6f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6275 :tutorial/title "Summarizing Confidence Intervals for Normal Populations With Known Population Variance"} :step/id #uuid "afbe0be3-1d3c-5408-9cda-36c61677f0fb" :step/math-academy-id 38032 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060767}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060769 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060968 :tutorial/content "In our example, the population is normally distributed. Therefore, the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ is also normally distributed, where\n\n$$\n\\overline{X}\\sim N\\left(\\mu,\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n}\\right).\n$$\n\nBy transforming $\\overline{X}$ to a standard normal random variable, we have\n\n$$\nZ = \\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\mu}{\\sigma/\\sqrt n}\\sim N(0,1).\n$$\n\nWe wish to find a $z$-interval that we're $95\\%$ confident that the random variable $Z$ lies within. This interval is indicated in the diagram below:\n\n![](images/50/50a0f49fe5c96ba3259832fbf1a0abcedffb342301ca069bb7b88d157a181019.png)\n\n  \n\nTo help with the notation, we define a new parameter $\\alpha.$ Since we're computing a $95\\%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.95=0.05\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ is precisely the area bounded by each \"tail\" that we're excluding from our confidence interval. Let's label the critical values at the endpoints of our interval as $\\pm z_{\\alpha/2}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/3c/3cdfd2c72bf7fe65e5d1b584d46d4c846e74615e54c739a6d1ea990c95011a8a.png)\n\n  \n\nAccording to our diagram,\n\n$$\nP(Z > z_{0.025}) = P(Z < -z_{0.025}) = 0.025.\n$$\n\n  \n\nUsing a percentage points table for the standard normal distribution, we find that $z_{0.025} \\approx 1.96.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(Z > 1.96) = P(Z < -1.96) = 0.025.\n$$\n\nIn other words,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-1.96<Z<1.96)=0.95.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, there is a $95\\%$ probability that our random variable $Z$ will lie in the interval $(-1.96, 1.96).$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We solve the inequality inside the parentheses of the last probability statement for the population mean $\\mu\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1.96< & \\,Z<1.96 \\\\ -1.96< & \\,\\frac{\\overset{―}{x}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}<1.96 \\\\ -1.96\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}< & \\,\\overset{―}{x}-\\mu <1.96\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}} \\\\ -\\overset{―}{x}-1.96\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}< & \\,-\\mu \\,<-\\overset{―}{x}+1.96\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}} \\\\ \\overset{―}{x}+1.96\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}> & \\,\\mu >\\overset{―}{x}-1.96\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}} \\\\ \\overset{―}{x}-1.96\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}< & \\,\\mu <\\overset{―}{x}+1.96\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\left(\\overline{x} - 1.96\\cdot\\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}},\\: \\overline{x} + 1.96\\cdot\\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}}\\right)\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting our values\n\n$$\nn=10, \\qquad\n\\overline{x}=12, \\qquad\n\\sigma=3,\n$$\n\nwe obtain that our $95\\%$ confidence interval for $\\mu$ is \n\n$$\n\\big( 12- 1.859, \\: 12 + 1.859 \\big).\n$$\n\n \n\nNow that we've seen how to construct a particular confidence interval, let's discuss the more general procedure." :tutorial/id #uuid "609f60a5-fbfd-5385-89e2-f80dd6281f39" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6398 :tutorial/title "Constructing Confidence Intervals"} :step/id #uuid "e659ed87-b26a-54f6-bb76-e98cf99a6b01" :step/math-academy-id 38686 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060768}}] :activity/title "Confidence Intervals for One Mean: Known Population Variance" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059935}]}
